Patch: zoneNameScan
Letter: L
Requires: C (zoneInfoCache)
Files: src/impl/locale.js, src/impl/util.js
Summary: parseZoneInfo: read the name from dtf.format() instead of formatToParts + find

L: the zone name is read the expensive way

Requires C (it anchors on C's parseZoneInfo). What C fixed was constructing a
formatter per value; what is left is how the name is got out of one, and that
is now ~74% of the cost of formatting a column that carries an abbreviation.

Two wastes, both in one expression. formatToParts() allocates a part object
per field — the formatter asks for six — and .find() then walks them running
toLowerCase() per part, to pull out one string. And the formatter it walks is
asking ICU to render a full date and time when the caller wants none of it.

So: one formatter carrying the name and nothing else but an h23 2-digit hour,
and plain format() on it. That leaves the name at a fixed position in a
fixed-width string, which is J's trick applied to the other Intl call — and
like J it establishes the layout with formatToParts once, at construction,
rather than assuming one. Three probes have to agree on where the name sits
(either side of a DST boundary, and one that would render a one-digit hour if
the locale ignored 2-digit); any disagreement, or a locale whose format()
output is not its parts joined, returns null and takes the stock path.

Measured at 7.39µs -> 1.56µs per lookup on node 26 (V8 14.6), and verified
against the stock expression over 2,049,840 instant/zone/locale/style
combinations: 13 zones including three with sub-hour offsets, 6 locales
including non-latn digits and a non-gregory calendar, all 6 timeZoneName
styles, zero mismatches and zero locales taking the fallback.

